{"vuln_data": {"id": 21, "subject": "libtiff", "bug_id": "bugzilla-2611", "source_file": "libtiff/tif_ojpeg.c", "line_numbers": [789], "failing_test": ["1.tif"], "passing_test": [], "count_neg": 1, "count_pos": 0, "binary_path": "tools/tiffmedian", "crash_input": "$POC foo", "exploit_file_list": ["tests/1.tif"], "crash_stack_trace": [["OJPEGDecodeRaw", "libtiff/tif_ojpeg.c", 816], ["OJPEGDecode", "libtiff/tif_ojpeg.c", 791], ["TIFFReadScanline", "libtiff/tif_read.c", 304], ["quant", "tools/tiffmedian.c", 771], ["main", "tools/tiffmedian.c", 283]], "test_timeout": 5, "language": "c", "config_script": "config.sh", "build_script": "build.sh", "test_script": "test.sh", "build_command": "make -j20 CFLAGS='-static' CPPFLAGS='-static' LDFLAGS='-static'", "bug_type": "Divide by Zero"}, "patch_success": ["success", "", ""], "ret_code": false, "err_msg": "", "genpatch_candidate": [{"fix_strategy": {"fix_location": {"locations": [{"file_name": "libtiff/tif_ojpeg.c", "fix_line": 816, "start_line": 810, "end_line": 825, "function_name": "", "code": "uint8 sx,sy;\n\tif (cc%sp->bytes_per_line!=0)\n\t{\n\t\tTIFFErrorExt(tif->tif_clientdata,module,\"Fractional scanline not read\");\n\t\treturn(0);\n\t}\n\tassert(cc>0);", "original_code": "static int\nOJPEGDecodeRaw(TIFF* tif, uint8* buf, tmsize_t cc)\n{\n\tstatic const char module[]=\"OJPEGDecodeRaw\";\n\tOJPEGState* sp=(OJPEGState*)tif->tif_data;\n\tuint8* m;\n\ttmsize_t n;\n\tuint8* oy;\n\tuint8* ocb;\n\tuint8* ocr;\n\tuint8* p;\n\tuint32 q;\n\tuint8* r;\n\tuint8 sx,sy;\n\tif (cc%sp->bytes_per_line!=0)\n\t{\n\t\tTIFFErrorExt(tif->tif_clientdata,module,\"Fractional scanline not read\");\n\t\treturn(0);\n\t}\n\tassert(cc>0);\n\tm=buf;\n\tn=cc;\n\tdo\n\t{\n\t\tif (sp->subsampling_convert_state==0)\n\t\t{\n\t\t\tif (jpeg_read_raw_data_encap(sp,&(sp->libjpeg_jpeg_decompress_struct),sp->subsampling_convert_ycbcrimage,sp->subsampling_ver*8)==0)\n\t\t\t\treturn(0);\n\t\t}\n\t\toy=sp->subsampling_convert_ybuf+sp->subsampling_convert_state*sp->subsampling_ver*sp->subsampling_convert_ylinelen;\n\t\tocb=sp->subsampling_convert_cbbuf+sp->subsampling_convert_state*sp->subsampling_convert_clinelen;\n\t\tocr=sp->subsampling_convert_crbuf+sp->subsampling_convert_state*sp->subsampling_convert_clinelen;\n\t\tp=m;\n\t\tfor (q=0; q<sp->subsampling_convert_clinelenout; q++)\n\t\t{\n\t\t\tr=oy;\n\t\t\tfor (sy=0; sy<sp->subsampling_ver; sy++)\n\t\t\t{\n\t\t\t\tfor (sx=0; sx<sp->subsampling_hor; sx++)\n\t\t\t\t\t*p++=*r++;\n\t\t\t\tr+=sp->subsampling_convert_ylinelen-sp->subsampling_hor;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\toy+=sp->subsampling_hor;\n\t\t\t*p++=*ocb++;\n\t\t\t*p++=*ocr++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tsp->subsampling_convert_state++;\n\t\tif (sp->subsampling_convert_state==sp->subsampling_convert_clines)\n\t\t\tsp->subsampling_convert_state=0;\n\t\tm+=sp->bytes_per_line;\n\t\tn-=sp->bytes_per_line;\n\t} while(n>0);\n\treturn(1);\n}", "patched_code": "static int\nOJPEGDecodeRaw(TIFF* tif, uint8* buf, tmsize_t cc)\n{\n\tstatic const char module[]=\"OJPEGDecodeRaw\";\n\tOJPEGState* sp=(OJPEGState*)tif->tif_data;\n\tuint8* m;\n\ttmsize_t n;\n\tuint8* oy;\n\tuint8* ocb;\n\tuint8* ocr;\n\tuint8* p;\n\tuint32 q;\n// FIXME: Crash CWE-369: Divide By Zero\n \tuint8* r;\n\tuint8 sx,sy;\n\tif (sp->bytes_per_line <= 0)\n\t{\n\t\tTIFFErrorExt(tif->tif_clientdata,module,\"Invalid bytes_per_line value\");\n\t\treturn(0);\n\t}\n\tif (cc%sp->bytes_per_line!=0)\n\t{\n\t\tTIFFErrorExt(tif->tif_clientdata,module,\"Fractional scanline not read\");\n\t\treturn(0);\n\t}\n\tassert(cc>0);\n\tm=buf;\n\tn=cc;\n\tdo\n\t{\n\t\tif (sp->subsampling_convert_state==0)\n\t\t{\n\t\t\tif (jpeg_read_raw_data_encap(sp,&(sp->libjpeg_jpeg_decompress_struct),sp->subsampling_convert_ycbcrimage,sp->subsampling_ver*8)==0)\n\t\t\t\treturn(0);\n\t\t}\n\t\toy=sp->subsampling_convert_ybuf+sp->subsampling_convert_state*sp->subsampling_ver*sp->subsampling_convert_ylinelen;\n\t\tocb=sp->subsampling_convert_cbbuf+sp->subsampling_convert_state*sp->subsampling_convert_clinelen;\n\t\tocr=sp->subsampling_convert_crbuf+sp->subsampling_convert_state*sp->subsampling_convert_clinelen;\n\t\tp=m;\n\t\tfor (q=0; q<sp->subsampling_convert_clinelenout; q++)\n\t\t{\n\t\t\tr=oy;\n\t\t\tfor (sy=0; sy<sp->subsampling_ver; sy++)\n\t\t\t{\n\t\t\t\tfor (sx=0; sx<sp->subsampling_hor; sx++)\n\t\t\t\t\t*p++=*r++;\n\t\t\t\tr+=sp->subsampling_convert_ylinelen-sp->subsampling_hor;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\toy+=sp->subsampling_hor;\n\t\t\t*p++=*ocb++;\n\t\t\t*p++=*ocr++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tsp->subsampling_convert_state++;\n\t\tif (sp->subsampling_convert_state==sp->subsampling_convert_clines)\n\t\t\tsp->subsampling_convert_state=0;\n\t\tm+=sp->bytes_per_line;\n\t\tn-=sp->bytes_per_line;\n\t} while(n>0);\n\treturn(1);\n}", "func_def": "static int\nOJPEGDecodeRaw(TIFF* tif, uint8* buf, tmsize_t cc)", "func_ret": ["return(0);", "return(0);", "return(1);"]}], "rationale": "The fix is applied at the OJPEGDecodeRaw function at line 816 where the Floating Point Exception (FPE) occurs. This is the most direct point where the vulnerability manifests. Rather than deferring validation to earlier stages in the call chain (OJPEGDecode or TIFFReadScanline), fixing at this location ensures that OJPEG-specific parameters are validated immediately before use in arithmetic operations. This approach: (1) directly prevents the divide-by-zero condition that triggers the FPE, (2) maintains the security boundary at the OJPEG decoding layer where the parameters are specifically known, and (3) avoids unnecessary validation overhead in higher-level functions that may call OJPEGDecodeRaw with valid parameters.\"", "score": 3, "confidence": 8, "reliability_score": 10.5, "eval_rationale": "The LLM identified the correct function (OJPEGDecodeRaw) and general area (around line 816) where the divide-by-zero vulnerability occurs. However, the selected fix location (lines 810-825) contains only validation code for scanline alignment and does not address the actual divide-by-zero operation. The code snippet shows a check for \"cc%sp->bytes_per_line!=0\" which validates that the scanline data is properly aligned, but this is NOT the source of the FPE vulnerability. The actual divide-by-zero operation occurs at or near line 816 itself, likely involving JPEG sampling factors (sx, sy) or image dimensions that are used as divisors without validation. The LLM's rationale correctly identifies that validation should occur at the OJPEGDecodeRaw function level, but the selected code location does not contain the problematic division operation. A proper fix would need to identify the specific arithmetic operation causing the FPE (e.g., \"something / sx\" or \"something / sy\" or \"something / stride\") and add validation to ensure the divisor is non-zero BEFORE that operation. The selected location appears to be addressing a different validation concern (scanline alignment) rather than the divide-by-zero issue itself. This represents a fundamental mismatch between the identified vulnerability and the proposed fix location."}, "guideline": "CWE-369 Divide By Zero - Input Validation Guideline: Always validate that divisors are non-zero before performing division or modulo operations. Per SEI CERT C Coding Standard (INT33-C), perform appropriate checks on divisors prior to division. This includes checking integer values derived from untrusted input (such as TIFF file parameters) to ensure they are not zero before using them in arithmetic operations.", "description": "Direct Zero Check on Divisor: Add an explicit validation check immediately before the modulo operation at line 816 (cc%sp->bytes_per_line!=0). Insert a guard condition to verify that sp->bytes_per_line is greater than zero. If sp->bytes_per_line is zero or invalid, log an error message using TIFFErrorExt and return 0 to indicate failure. This prevents the Floating Point Exception by ensuring the divisor is always non-zero before the modulo operation executes. The fix should be applied as: if (sp->bytes_per_line <= 0) { TIFFErrorExt(tif->tif_clientdata, module, \"Invalid bytes_per_line value\"); return(0); } followed by the existing cc%sp->bytes_per_line check.", "rationale": "This fix directly addresses the root cause of the CWE-369 divide-by-zero vulnerability by adding a validation guard immediately before the dangerous arithmetic operation. By checking sp->bytes_per_line > 0 before using it as a divisor in the modulo operation, the fix prevents the Floating Point Exception from occurring. The error handling is consistent with existing libtiff patterns (using TIFFErrorExt) and provides clear diagnostic information about why the TIFF file cannot be processed. This targeted fix has minimal performance impact and affects only the vulnerable code path.", "score": 8, "confidence": 8, "reliability_score": 14.4, "eval_rationale": "The LLM's proposed fix strategy demonstrates a strong understanding of the CWE-369 divide-by-zero vulnerability and provides an appropriate, well-reasoned solution. Here's my detailed evaluation:\n\n**Strengths:**\n\n1. **Correct Vulnerability Identification**: The LLM accurately identifies that the vulnerability is a divide-by-zero issue occurring in the modulo operation at line 816 (cc%sp->bytes_per_line!=0). The root cause analysis correctly points to insufficient validation of the divisor sp->bytes_per_line.\n\n2. **Appropriate Fix Strategy**: The proposed fix\u2014adding a validation check before the modulo operation to ensure sp->bytes_per_line > 0\u2014directly addresses the root cause. This is the standard and correct approach for CWE-369 vulnerabilities per SEI CERT C Coding Standard (INT33-C).\n\n3. **Proper Error Handling**: The fix maintains consistency with existing libtiff patterns by using TIFFErrorExt for error reporting and returning 0 to indicate failure, which is appropriate for the codebase.\n\n4. **Correct Fix Location**: The proposed fix location (lines 810-825) aligns well with the vulnerability location. The fix is placed immediately before the dangerous operation, which is the correct placement for a guard condition.\n\n5. **Clear Rationale**: The explanation of why this fix works is sound\u2014by validating the divisor before use, the Floating Point Exception is prevented.\n\n6. **Minimal Impact**: The fix is targeted and should not affect other features of the project, as it only adds a validation check in the vulnerable code path.\n\n**Weaknesses/Limitations:**\n\n1. **Slightly Overly Specific**: While the fix is appropriate, the LLM provides a very specific code snippet (if (sp->bytes_per_line <= 0) { ... }) without acknowledging that the exact implementation details may vary slightly depending on the broader context of the function. However, this is a minor issue as the core logic is sound.\n\n2. **Limited Discussion of Alternative Fixes**: The response doesn't mention that there might be other related divisors in the function that could also be zero (though the focus on sp->bytes_per_line appears correct based on the provided vulnerability information).\n\n3. **No Discussion of Upstream Validation**: The fix doesn't discuss whether validation could be performed earlier in the call chain (TIFFReadScanline \u2192 OJPEGDecode \u2192 OJPEGDecodeRaw), though this is a minor point as the immediate fix is still necessary.\n\n**Alignment with Vulnerability Information:**\n\n- The vulnerability comprehension states the issue occurs \"during Old JPEG (OJPEG) decoding when processing TIFF image data\" with \"zero values in critical JPEG parameters\"\n- The fix directly addresses this by validating sp->bytes_per_line before use\n- The fix location aligns with the code provided (lines 810-825)\n- The error handling approach is consistent with libtiff patterns\n\n**Applicability:**\n\nThe proposed fix is clearly applicable to the specified code location and should resolve the vulnerability without breaking existing functionality. The validation check is straightforward and follows established secure coding practices.\n\nOverall, this is a high-quality response that correctly identifies the vulnerability, proposes an appropriate fix, and provides sound reasoning. The minor limitations prevent it from being a perfect 9-10 score."}, "patch_result": "success"}, {"fix_strategy": {"fix_location": {"locations": [{"file_name": "libtiff/tif_ojpeg.c", "fix_line": 816, "start_line": 810, "end_line": 825, "function_name": "", "code": "uint8 sx,sy;\n\tif (cc%sp->bytes_per_line!=0)\n\t{\n\t\tTIFFErrorExt(tif->tif_clientdata,module,\"Fractional scanline not read\");\n\t\treturn(0);\n\t}\n\tassert(cc>0);", "original_code": "static int\nOJPEGDecodeRaw(TIFF* tif, uint8* buf, tmsize_t cc)\n{\n\tstatic const char module[]=\"OJPEGDecodeRaw\";\n\tOJPEGState* sp=(OJPEGState*)tif->tif_data;\n\tuint8* m;\n\ttmsize_t n;\n\tuint8* oy;\n\tuint8* ocb;\n\tuint8* ocr;\n\tuint8* p;\n\tuint32 q;\n\tuint8* r;\n\tuint8 sx,sy;\n\tif (cc%sp->bytes_per_line!=0)\n\t{\n\t\tTIFFErrorExt(tif->tif_clientdata,module,\"Fractional scanline not read\");\n\t\treturn(0);\n\t}\n\tassert(cc>0);\n\tm=buf;\n\tn=cc;\n\tdo\n\t{\n\t\tif (sp->subsampling_convert_state==0)\n\t\t{\n\t\t\tif (jpeg_read_raw_data_encap(sp,&(sp->libjpeg_jpeg_decompress_struct),sp->subsampling_convert_ycbcrimage,sp->subsampling_ver*8)==0)\n\t\t\t\treturn(0);\n\t\t}\n\t\toy=sp->subsampling_convert_ybuf+sp->subsampling_convert_state*sp->subsampling_ver*sp->subsampling_convert_ylinelen;\n\t\tocb=sp->subsampling_convert_cbbuf+sp->subsampling_convert_state*sp->subsampling_convert_clinelen;\n\t\tocr=sp->subsampling_convert_crbuf+sp->subsampling_convert_state*sp->subsampling_convert_clinelen;\n\t\tp=m;\n\t\tfor (q=0; q<sp->subsampling_convert_clinelenout; q++)\n\t\t{\n\t\t\tr=oy;\n\t\t\tfor (sy=0; sy<sp->subsampling_ver; sy++)\n\t\t\t{\n\t\t\t\tfor (sx=0; sx<sp->subsampling_hor; sx++)\n\t\t\t\t\t*p++=*r++;\n\t\t\t\tr+=sp->subsampling_convert_ylinelen-sp->subsampling_hor;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\toy+=sp->subsampling_hor;\n\t\t\t*p++=*ocb++;\n\t\t\t*p++=*ocr++;\n\t\t}\n\t\tsp->subs